INT. WAX MUSEUM - AFTER HOURS - NIGHT

A wax museum at midnight. Rows of lifelike figures in period costume, glass eyes catching the exit light. A velvet rope sags. A faint scent of melting paraffin hangs in the dark.

INSPECTOR HOLLIS DRAKE, fifties, trench-coated, a pocket flashlight in his teeth, crouches by a figure of Napoleon. DR. WILHELMINA VOSS, forties, the museum curator, stands over him with a clipboard. CONSTABLE REUBEN OKAFOR-LEE, twenties, shifts his weight at the door, hand on his truncheon. MADAME ODILE FERRIER, seventies, the sculptor, sits on a folding stool gripping a cane. LORD ASHTON PEMBERTON, sixties, the patron, leans against a plinth, his cravat undone. NURSE JOSEPHINE BELL, thirties, hovers at his shoulder with a medical bag.

DRAKE
(to Wilhelmina)
When was this figure last moved?

WILHELMINA
Never. It's been bolted since 1962.

DRAKE
Then who changed his hat?

WILHELMINA
I'm sorry?

He stands. He lifts the bicorne from Napoleon's head. Beneath it, the wax has been scored with a fine hairline crack, and in the crack, something glints.

DRAKE
Constable, gloves.

REUBEN
Here, sir.

He hands them over. Drake slides a gloved finger into the crack, hooks the glint, and draws it out: a small brass key.

PEMBERTON
Well, there you are. A key. How delightful.

ODILE
(sharply)
Ashton.

PEMBERTON
What, Odile?

ODILE
You promised.

Pemberton straightens. Josephine's hand drifts to his sleeve. He shakes it off.

DRAKE
Lord Pemberton, you've seen this key before.

PEMBERTON
I haven't.

DRAKE
You have a matching one in your waistcoat.

Pemberton's hand goes to his chest. Odile bows her head. Wilhelmina lowers her clipboard.

WILHELMINA
Inspector, what does the key open?

DRAKE
Dr. Voss, I believe you can tell me.

WILHELMINA
(a long pause)
The cellar. Under the Chamber of Horrors.

DRAKE
And what's in the cellar?

WILHELMINA
The first sculpture. The one Madame Ferrier never exhibited.

All eyes turn to Odile. She sets down her cane. She lifts her chin.

ODILE
I made her so well, Inspector. So very well. Nobody could tell she wasn't alive.

DRAKE
Who was she?

ODILE
My sister.

REUBEN
Ma'am?

ODILE
She sat for me on the last night of her life. I could not bear to let her go.

JOSEPHINE
(whispering to Pemberton)
You knew?

PEMBERTON
(low)
I paid to keep it quiet.

DRAKE
Constable, escort Madame Ferrier and Lord Pemberton to the carriage. Nurse Bell, you will assist Dr. Voss with the cellar.

REUBEN
Yes, sir.

PHONE (V.O.)
(from the hallway)
Inspector, this is the Commissioner. Have you concluded?

DRAKE
(calling toward the hall)
Nearly, sir.

Drake pockets the key. Odile rises with surprising grace and takes Reuben's arm. At the door she stops, turns, and looks at the figures one last time.

ODILE
They keep better than we do, you know.

She exits. The wax faces stare after her. Drake switches off his flashlight and the museum goes dim.
